          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:15] Recall:</strong> How many Palestinian Arab villages were depopulated or destroyed during the 1948 war, according to historical documentation?</li><li><strong>[02:50] Explain:</strong> Explain how events like the Deir Yassin massacre in April 1948 and military operations under Plan Dalet caused widespread civilian panic and flight.</li><li><strong>[04:40] Challenge:</strong> Analyze the historiographical debate between traditional Israeli historians (who argued Arabs left voluntarily on orders from Arab radio broadcasts) and New Historians (such as Benny Morris). What do archival records reveal?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Over 400 Palestinian Arab villages (historians estimate between 418 and 530 villages).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The massacre of over 100 villagers in Deir Yassin by Irgun and Lehi fighters was widely broadcast on radio, generating terror among civilian populations. Under Plan Dalet (April 1948), Haganah commanders cleared strategic roads and border villages to prevent Arab forces from severing supply lines to Jewish Jerusalem, resulting in direct expulsions (such as at Lydda and Ramle) and spontaneous panic flight.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Archival research by Benny Morris and others proved that no Arab radio broadcasts instructed civilians to flee; rather, mass flight was triggered by military assault, psychological warfare, fear of atrocities, and in dozens of cases, direct expulsion orders signed by Israeli commanders. Morris concluded that the refugee problem was 'born of war, not by design, but also not entirely by accident'.</li></ol>"
