          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:05] Recall:</strong> What island hosted the 1949 UN-brokered armistice talks between Israel and neighboring Arab states, and what color ink was used to draw the boundary lines?</li><li><strong>[02:30] Explain:</strong> Explain how the 1949 Armistice Agreements altered the territorial division of Palestine compared to the 1947 UN Partition Plan (Resolution 181).</li><li><strong>[04:10] Challenge:</strong> Why did the 1949 Armistice Agreements intentionally avoid establishing permanent international borders, and how did this ambiguity ensure continued regional conflict?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The Greek island of Rhodes (brokered by UN mediator Ralph Bunche), and green ink (giving rise to the term 'The Green Line').</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Under UN Resolution 181, Israel was allocated 55% of Palestine, and Arabs 44%. By the 1949 armistices, Israel expanded its control to 78%. The proposed independent Palestinian Arab state was completely eliminated: Jordan annexed the West Bank and East Jerusalem, while Egypt occupied the Gaza Strip.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The armistice agreements were strictly military ceasefires, explicitly stating that the Green Line was not an internationally recognized political border. Arab states refused to sign formal peace treaties that would legitimize Israel's existence, while Israel refused to relinquish captured territory or accept Palestinian refugees, creating a temporary truce that guaranteed future wars.</li></ol>"
