          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:10] Recall:</strong> What international peacekeeping force did President Nasser order to immediately evacuate the Sinai border on 16 May 1967?</li><li><strong>[02:40] Explain:</strong> Why did the State of Israel consider President Nasser's closure of the Straits of Tiran to Israeli shipping on 22 May 1967 an explicit act of war (<em>casus belli</em>)?</li><li><strong>[04:20] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate the role of political brinkmanship: did President Nasser genuinely intend to launch an unprovoked invasion of Israel, or did he become a prisoner of his own public rhetoric and regional Arab rivalry?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The United Nations Emergency Force (UNEF), stationed in Sinai since the 1956 Suez Crisis.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The Straits of Tiran were Israel's sole maritime outlet to the Red Sea and Indian Ocean, vital for imported Persian crude oil through the port of Eilat. Following 1956, international maritime guarantees had established that closing the straits was an act of war. The blockade severed Israel's economic lifeline and forced the government to mobilize 250,000 civilian reservists, paralyzing its economy.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Declassified records suggest Nasser was engaging in political theater to reassert Pan-Arab leadership after being mocked by Jordan and Saudi Arabia for hiding behind UN skirts. However, by massing 100,000 troops, expelling UNEF, blockading the Straits, and signing defense pacts with Jordan and Iraq, Nasser created a military stranglehold that forced Israel to strike pre-emptively to survive.</li></ol>"
