          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:30] Recall:</strong> What major engineering project did Israel complete in 1964 to pump water to the arid Negev desert, and what counter-scheme was launched by the Arab League?</li><li><strong>[03:15] Explain:</strong> Explain how Syrian military positions on the high cliffs of the Golan Heights held a commanding tactical advantage over Israeli agricultural kibbutzim in the Hula Valley below.</li><li><strong>[04:50] Challenge:</strong> To what extent was the outbreak of the Six-Day War driven by competition over vital ecological resources (freshwater) rather than purely ideological or religious hostility?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Israel completed the National Water Carrier; the Arab League launched the Headwater Diversion Scheme to divert the Hasbani and Banias tributaries away from Israel.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The Golan Heights rose steeply hundreds of meters above northern Israeli settlements. Syrian artillery batteries heavily entrenched in basalt bunkers could shell civilian farmers, tractors, and fishing boats on the Sea of Galilee with near-total impunity, triggering retaliatory Israeli airstrikes and tank duels.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Freshwater was existential: diverting the headwaters would have cut Israel's water supply by 35%, halting agricultural and demographic development. While nationalist and anti-Zionist rhetoric dominated the airwaves, the continuous physical combat over water bulldozers and fishing rights between 1964 and 1967 served as the direct operational sparks that escalated border skirmishes into all-out war.</li></ol>"
