          "pathway": "Strategic Overview",
          "teacher_guidance": "Objective, non-partisan military analysis explaining the collapse of Egyptian forces in the Sinai passes, Jordanian artillery shelling of Tel Aviv and Israeli counter-attack, and the storming of the Golan Heights. Recommended Watch Window: 00:00–08:30 • Lesson Phase: Guided Instruction & Dual-Coding. Tone Alert: Objective, detailed military timeline breaking down operational movements across all three combat fronts.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[02:10] Recall:</strong> On which three distinct geographical fronts did the Israel Defense Forces fight between 5 and 10 June 1967?</li><li><strong>[04:45] Explain:</strong> Why did Egyptian Field Marshal Abdel Hakim Amer issue a catastrophic general retreat order across the Sinai on 6 June, and what were its military consequences?</li><li><strong>[07:15] Challenge:</strong> Analyze the factors behind Israeli military dominance: how did superior pilot training, ground turnaround speed (jets refueled and rearmed in under 8 minutes), and decentralized officer initiative explain Israel's victory over numerically larger Arab armies?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The Southern Front in the Sinai Peninsula and Gaza against Egypt; the Eastern Front in the West Bank and Jerusalem against Jordan; and the Northern Front on the Golan Heights against Syria.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Panicked by the total destruction of his air force and initial IDF breakthroughs, Amer suffered an emotional breakdown and ordered a disorderly retreat to the Suez Canal without organizing rear-guard defensive lines. Egyptian divisions abandoned their heavy equipment in panic, turning the narrow Sinai mountain passes (such as Mitla) into deadly bottlenecks where hundreds of tanks were destroyed.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Arab armies were strictly hierarchical, Soviet-trained, and politically distrustful, requiring rigid top-down orders before acting. Conversely, Israeli pilots averaged hundreds more flight hours, ground crews turned around warplanes four times faster than standard NATO times, and junior Israeli commanders were trained to exploit tactical initiative on the spot, creating irresistible operational tempo.</li></ol>"
