          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[02:30] Recall:</strong> Which brigade of Israeli paratroopers breached the Lions' Gate to capture the Old City of Jerusalem on 7 June 1967, and who commanded them?</li><li><strong>[04:15] Explain:</strong> Why did the capture of the Old City and the Western Wall evoke such an extraordinary outpouring of tears and euphoria across the Jewish world?</li><li><strong>[05:50] Challenge:</strong> Deconstruct the term 'reunification of Jerusalem'. How do Palestinians and international bodies view the annexation of East Jerusalem under international law, and why does this remain one of the most explosive issues in the world?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The 55th Paratroopers Brigade, commanded by Colonel Mordechai 'Motta' Gur.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> For nearly 2,000 years since the Roman destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE—and specifically during the 19 years of Jordanian rule (1948–1967) when Jews were barred from the Old City—the Western Wall was inaccessible. When battle-hardened paratroopers reached the Wall and heard the Shofar blown, it felt like the historic fulfillment of two millennia of prayer and survival.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> While Israel passed legislation declaring Jerusalem 'complete and united' as its eternal capital, Palestinians view East Jerusalem as the occupied capital of their future state. Under international law (including UN Security Council Resolution 252 and the Fourth Geneva Convention), unilateral annexation of territory by war is illegal, leaving Jerusalem's sovereign status internationally contested.</li></ol>"
