          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:10] Recall:</strong> In what month and year was UN Security Council Resolution 242 unanimously adopted, and what was the name of the British UN ambassador who drafted its delicate text?</li><li><strong>[02:35] Explain:</strong> Explain the diplomatic architecture of the 'Land for Peace' formula created by Resolution 242.</li><li><strong>[03:50] Challenge:</strong> Analyze the crucial linguistic loophole between the English version ('withdrawal from territories occupied') and the French version ('retrait des territoires occupés'). Why did this linguistic ambiguity cause decades of diplomatic stalemate?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> November 1967; drafted by British UN Ambassador Lord Caradon (Hugh Foot).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Resolution 242 paired the 'inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by war' with the right of every state in the region 'to live in peace within secure and recognized boundaries'. In essence, Israel would return captured lands in exchange for full Arab diplomatic recognition, peace treaties, and guaranteed security.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The English text omitted the definite article 'the' before 'territories', allowing Israel to argue it was only obligated to make partial withdrawals to defensible borders. The French text included 'des' (implying all territories), allowing Arab states to demand total withdrawal from every inch of 1967 land as a precondition for talks. Lord Caradon admitted this ambiguity was deliberate to secure unanimous agreement.</li></ol>"
