          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[00:55] Recall:</strong> What are the famous 'Three Noes' formulated by Arab leaders at the August 1967 Arab League summit in Khartoum, Sudan?</li><li><strong>[02:00] Explain:</strong> Why did Arab leaders adopt such a maximalist, unyielding stance at Khartoum despite their catastrophic territorial losses in Sinai, the West Bank, and the Golan?</li><li><strong>[03:10] Challenge:</strong> How did the Khartoum Resolution's 'Three Noes' reinforce Israeli domestic arguments that the captured territories should not be returned, cementing the diplomatic stalemate?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> 'No peace with Israel, no recognition of Israel, no negotiations with it.'</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Arab leaders met in a state of profound shock and humiliation. Agreeing to negotiate from a position of total military defeat would have been viewed domestically as surrender. To preserve regional solidarity and regime survival, the Arab League banded together to prove that military setback did not mean political submission.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Prime Minister Levi Eshkol and Defence Minister Moshe Dayan had initially signaled willingness to return most of Sinai and the Golan Heights in exchange for bilateral peace treaties ('waiting for a telephone call from Arab leaders'). The 'Three Noes' convinced the Israeli public that Arab hostility was immutable, accelerating plans to retain the conquered territories as permanent military buffers.</li></ol>"
