          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[00:40] Recall:</strong> What forms of non-violent civil disobedience did local Palestinian popular committees organize during the First Intifada?</li><li><strong>[01:15] Explain:</strong> How did the First Intifada represent a radical transformation from armed guerrilla warfare directed by exiled leaders to popular grassroots resistance led from within?</li><li><strong>[01:50] Challenge:</strong> Analyze the crucial role played by Palestinian women's committees during the uprising: why is their leadership frequently overlooked in traditional military accounts?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Commercial strikes, refusing to pay Israeli taxes, boycotting Israeli-made consumer goods, setting up clandestine agricultural cooperatives, and establishing secret underground classrooms when schools were shuttered.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The PLO leadership was stranded thousands of miles away in Tunis following the 1982 Lebanon war. The Intifada was launched and organized by ordinary residents, university students, women's committees, and trade unionists (the Unified National Leadership of the Uprising), proving that Palestinians inside the territories did not need external military commands to fight for freedom.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> When tens of thousands of Palestinian men were arrested and held in detention camps without trial, women stepped into leadership: managing food distribution, organizing alternative underground schools, and mobilizing street protests. Traditional military histories focus on male politicians and stone-throwers, erasing the sophisticated social organizing that sustained the uprising for six years.</li></ol>"
