          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:30] Recall:</strong> Under the 1995 Oslo II Interim Agreement, which administrative zone (Area A, B, or C) encompassed 60% of the West Bank and remained under full Israeli civil and military control?</li><li><strong>[03:00] Explain:</strong> Explain the operational division of authority between Area A (Palestinian civil and security control), Area B (Palestinian civil, joint Israeli-Palestinian security control), and Area C (full Israeli control).</li><li><strong>[04:20] Challenge:</strong> Why did the architects of the Oslo Accords intentionally postpone the most contentious 'final status' issues (Jerusalem, refugees, borders, and settlements) for five years, and how did this delay allow extremists to destroy the process?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Area C (encompassing roughly 60% of the West Bank, including all Israeli settlements, military bases, and bypass roads).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Area A covered major Palestinian cities (Ramallah, Nablus, Bethlehem) under full Palestinian Authority control. Area B covered roughly 440 Palestinian villages under PA civil administration but with Israeli overriding security control. Area C covered all agricultural lands, border crossings, and settlements under exclusive Israeli military administration.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Negotiators believed that jumping straight into impossible disputes like Jerusalem or the Right of Return would immediately collapse the talks; they hoped five years of successful interim cooperation would build mutual trust. Instead, the delay provided a five-year window for extremists on both sides to wage terror campaigns, expand settlements, and poison public trust.</li></ol>"
